title: '4. How hard a plan is checked is set by signs anyone can count: the planner counts them, Tay may raise the tier but never lower it, and Top only looks it up.'
pid: 7e712327-58f6-46c1-afc7-0ab909b7c33f
body: |2-
  The signs, asked of the plan as written:
  - one-way doors among its decisions (step 1 says what counts);
  - territories it changes, and whether it crosses a border that does
    not follow files;
  - building missions it needs, and whether any is built by a
    specialist other than the author's;
  - questions it leaves for Jeff that Tay cannot answer.
  They set the tier, and the tier sets the checking:
  - none: no plan (step 1). The building mission's check and its
    fresh subagent (step 7) are all.
  - Tay: one territory, one building mission by the author's own
    specialist, no one-way door.
  - Tay, then Libby: a border crossed, or more than one building
    mission, or a builder other than the author's specialist.
  - Tay, Libby, and Jeff: any one-way door, or a question Tay cannot
    answer from Jeff's taste. Jeff hears only those decisions, never
    the whole plan (step 6).
  The planner writes the counts and the tier in the plan's first
  lines, beside its state. Top reads those lines, not the plan, and
  briefs the readers the tier names. Tay reads every plan, so Tay
  checks the count; Tay may raise the tier, and lowering it takes
  Top's word. Every judgement here is a count, so judging costs no
  mission and no more than a minute of anyone's reading.
  So the judges are Top for the request, the planner for the plan,
  and Tay for the count: no new specialist, and no judging mission.
  Why, in [[id:7c1c9b52-05d2-4d67-8891-b1f937b06b39][where the planning procedure departs from its sources]].
  Rests on: read (ITIL's change enablement: approval is set by risk,
  low-risk standard changes need no sign-off, and not every change
  goes to one board; Bezos 2015: the heavy process used for
  everything makes an organization slow; Anthropic's research
  system: agents misjudge how much effort a task deserves, so the
  rules for scaling it are written out; Rust's list of what is
  "substantial"). Ours: the signs, the tiers, who counts. ? (unsure:
  whether a planner counts its own plan honestly.)
